WEBVTT

00:00:00.476 --> 00:00:02.873
Imagine a robot with two buttons.

00:00:02.992 --> 00:00:07.771
One reliably moves a puck left, and the other reliably moves it right.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:12.497
After seeing the puck, an observer can infer which button was pressed.

00:00:12.616 --> 00:00:16.220
The robot has transmitted one bit through the physical world.

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:19.277
That is the basic intuition behind empowerment.

00:00:19.574 --> 00:00:24.372
Now disconnect the buttons and let a fan randomly blow the puck left or right.

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:26.988
The outcome still has one bit of entropy.

00:00:27.107 --> 00:00:30.066
Yet it tells us nothing about the chosen button.

00:00:30.185 --> 00:00:36.787
Subtracting the uncertainty that remains after the input is known removes this uncontrollable randomness.

00:00:36.906 --> 00:00:39.339
What remains is mutual information.

00:00:39.636 --> 00:00:47.487
A learned skill can replace a single button: choose a latent variable, run a policy, and observe a future state.

00:00:47.606 --> 00:00:53.989
Maximizing information over the input distribution gives the capacity of that particular channel.

00:00:54.108 --> 00:00:59.048
The available skills, the observation, and the time horizon all matter.

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:03.091
We will use this communication picture to understand DIAYN.

00:01:04.458 --> 00:01:08.609
In DIAYN, we first draw a skill label from a fixed prior.

00:01:08.728 --> 00:01:13.105
The same policy network receives both the current state and this label.

00:01:13.224 --> 00:01:16.408
Hold the label fixed while collecting a trajectory.

00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:24.832
Different labels should produce state distributions that an observer can distinguish, without being told a downstream task reward.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:29.598
Our diagram has three colored skills exploring different parts of a room.

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:36.575
If every color visits exactly the same distribution, the state reveals no information about the label.

00:01:36.694 --> 00:01:41.853
If the distributions become distinct, the label becomes easier to decode.

00:01:41.972 --> 00:01:48.016
This is a statement about distributions, not just three hand-picked trajectory endpoints.

00:01:48.314 --> 00:01:53.748
DIAYN also rewards entropy in the action distribution conditioned on the state and skill.

00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:58.199
That encourages flexible behavior within each distinguishable skill.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:05.111
The canonical information objective combines state-skill mutual information with this action entropy term.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:14.060
High action entropy alone would just produce randomness; the state discriminator supplies the pressure toward identifiable effects.

00:02:15.428 --> 00:02:20.282
Here is the mathematical bridge from information theory to reinforcement learning.

00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:28.278
Write mutual information as the expected log posterior probability of the skill, minus its log prior probability.

00:02:28.397 --> 00:02:30.248
The prior is chosen by us.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:36.179
The posterior would require knowing the complete distributions generated by the policies.

00:02:36.476 --> 00:02:40.143
Replace that posterior with a trainable classifier.

00:02:40.262 --> 00:02:44.469
Add and subtract its log probability inside the expectation.

00:02:44.588 --> 00:02:51.284
The leftover term is an expected Kullback Leibler divergence from the true posterior to the classifier.

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:58.697
Because this divergence cannot be negative, the classifier expression is a lower bound on the information we wanted.

00:02:58.994 --> 00:03:05.232
This gives a concrete intrinsic reward: log classifier probability minus log prior.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.689
A state earns reward when it is stronger evidence for the sampled skill than the prior alone.

00:03:10.808 --> 00:03:14.067
The bound becomes tight with a correct classifier.

00:03:14.185 --> 00:03:21.758
Otherwise, improving the classifier and improving the policy address different sources of error in the same objective.

00:03:23.125 --> 00:03:27.576
Let us compute the reward instead of treating the formula as decoration.

00:03:27.695 --> 00:03:30.790
Suppose there are four equally probable skills.

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:35.801
Before observing a state, the probability of the chosen skill is one quarter.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:44.945
If the discriminator assigns probability point eight to the correct label afterward, the reward is log three point two, about one point

00:03:45.064 --> 00:03:46.378
one six nats.

00:03:46.675 --> 00:03:51.904
If the classifier stays at chance, the ratio is one and the reward is zero.

00:03:52.023 --> 00:03:59.060
If it gives the true skill probability point zero five, the ratio becomes point two and the reward is negative.

00:03:59.179 --> 00:04:07.531
The policy is rewarded for evidence about its actual label, not simply for producing any confident classifier prediction.

00:04:07.828 --> 00:04:12.371
The lab generalizes this example into a noisy categorical channel.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:19.845
Change how much the skill distributions overlap and compare actual mutual information with the variational bound.

00:04:19.964 --> 00:04:25.896
A miscalibrated decoder can lower the bound even when the underlying skills remain distinct.

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:31.632
This is a controlled calculation, not a replication of a locomotion benchmark.

00:04:32.999 --> 00:04:35.761
The learning loop now has two coupled parts.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:42.095
Sample a label, execute the label-conditioned policy, and store states with their labels.

00:04:42.214 --> 00:04:49.391
Those labels are free supervision: they came from the experimenter, rather than an external reward function.

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:53.426
A discriminator learns to predict them from the observed states.

00:04:53.723 --> 00:05:00.538
Next, use the discriminator reward inside an entropy-regularized reinforcement learning update.

00:05:00.656 --> 00:05:08.447
Better policies can produce more separable states, which give the discriminator a clearer classification problem.

00:05:08.566 --> 00:05:13.187
A more accurate discriminator gives the policy a more faithful signal.

00:05:13.306 --> 00:05:20.366
Neither update makes the other automatically correct, especially with limited data or function approximation.

00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:26.747
A practical implementation must keep the skill identity attached to each replay transition.

00:05:26.866 --> 00:05:34.248
It must also separate the task reward used for later evaluation from the intrinsic reward used during pretraining.

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:40.545
Otherwise an apparently unsupervised result can quietly depend on downstream supervision.

00:05:40.664 --> 00:05:49.011
Our included code computes the information objective exactly, rather than pretending to reproduce the entire training system.

00:05:50.379 --> 00:05:56.150
The useful product is a repertoire: a skill-conditioned policy together with a decoder.

00:05:56.269 --> 00:06:01.745
A later task can select skills, compose them, or fine-tune their parameters.

00:06:01.864 --> 00:06:06.963
But distinguishable behavior is not a promise that every useful task is covered.

00:06:07.082 --> 00:06:12.315
The features supplied to the discriminator influence which differences are worth learning.

00:06:12.612 --> 00:06:14.626
There is another subtle distinction.

00:06:14.745 --> 00:06:17.930
Executing a skill may consume an opportunity.

00:06:18.049 --> 00:06:25.900
A robot can demonstrate that it knows several ways to leave a platform, while ending up somewhere with few remaining choices.

00:06:26.019 --> 00:06:34.127
Measuring information transmitted by a chosen skill set and seeking states with many available skills are different optimization

00:06:34.246 --> 00:06:35.058
problems.

00:06:35.356 --> 00:06:38.901
Keep three questions separate as the series continues.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:41.246
What behaviors are distinguishable?

00:06:41.364 --> 00:06:44.914
From which starting states can they actually be executed?

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:47.265
And who benefits from those choices?

00:06:47.384 --> 00:06:51.037
DIAYN provides our starting language for the first question.

00:06:51.156 --> 00:06:59.108
The geometry and scaling papers address the second; the assistance papers change the agent whose control we care about.
